% ============================================================================
% initial-sound — a word's beginning sound (phoneme), a THIRD literacy
% sub-skill in the ADJ stdlib (adj-facts-stdlib, LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% Deliberately DIFFERENT in shape from both prior literacy libraries:
% `word-families.adj` derives RHYMING (RF.K.2.a, shared END sound) via a
% `rule`, and `syllable-count.adj` recalls a SYLLABLE COUNT (RF.K.2.b). This
% library recalls a word's BEGINNING sound (phoneme identity/isolation,
% RF.K.2.d — "Isolate and pronounce the initial ... sound(s) in ... spoken
% words") as a pure lookup: `initial_sound(word, sound)`. Recall is a
% binding query:
%
%     ? initial_sound(bell, $S)   % b
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `initial_sound(word, sound)` carrying the Reading Rockets citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% sound WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on
% any word not one of the three the cited sentence names. The query also
% runs BACKWARD — bind a sound and recall every word sharing it:
%
%     ? initial_sound($W, b)   % bell, bike, boy (all three)
%
% This backward direction is itself the classic "phoneme identity" exercise
% Reading Rockets describes: "Recognizing which words in a set of words
% begin with the same sound."
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from Reading Rockets' "Reading 101 for
% Parents: Phonological and Phonemic Awareness" guide, WebFetch-verified
% TWICE for consistency before writing (the same sentence also appears,
% word-for-word, on at least one sibling Reading Rockets page, confirming
% it is the site's own standing canonical example rather than a one-off).
% The exact sentence: "Bell, bike, and boy all have /b/ at the beginning."
% — appearing under a "Children can demonstrate phonemic awareness in
% several ways, including: Recognizing which words in a set of words begin
% with the same sound" bullet. `trust consensus` — a widely-used literacy
% parent/educator resource, the same tier `syllable-count.adj`'s Reading
% Rockets source earned.
%
% Deliberately scoped to ONLY the three words (bell, bike, boy) and the one
% sound (/b/) this single cited sentence names — all three happen to share
% one phoneme, so the table is honestly narrow (mirroring
% `syllable-count.adj`'s all-2-syllable table) rather than fabricating a
% second phoneme group from an uncited word list. A future slice can add a
% second sound group once a comparably clean citation is found. (See
% ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table initial_sound {
    columns word, sound

    row (bell, b)
    row (bike, b)
    row (boy, b)

    source "Bell, bike, and boy all have /b/ at the beginning."
    locator "https://www.readingrockets.org/literacy-home/reading-101-guide-parents/reading-basics/phonological-and-phonemic-awareness"
    trust consensus
}
